
This project implements the dataplane of an IPv4 router. It focuses on Ethernet and IPv4 packet processing, checksum verification, TTL handling, ARP, routing table lookup, and ICMP message generation.

IPv4 Forwarding

When a packet is received, the router first checks if it is an IPv4 packet and verifies that the checksum is valid. Then, it searches for the best route to the destination, checks and decrements the TTL, and recomputes the checksum.
To determine the MAC address of the next hop, the router uses the ARP table. After updating the Ethernet header with the correct source and destination MAC addresses, the packet is forwarded.
In the `subiect1.png` screenshot, a ping is sent from `h-1` to `192.168.0.2`, and the terminal shows that the reply is received correctly. In Wireshark, the ARP packets, ICMP Echo Request, and ICMP Echo Reply packets can be seen. The TTL decreases from 64 to 63, which shows that the packet was forwarded correctly.

Longest Prefix Match

This part improves the route lookup by replacing the linear search with a trie-based implementation. Each route is inserted into the trie based on its prefix and mask length, using the `add_route` and `build_trie` functions.
The prefix length is computed using `get_mask_len`, and each route is added to the trie by moving either to the left or to the right branch, depending on the bits of the prefix. In the `best_route` function, the search starts from the root, and the bits of the destination IP address are processed from left to right.
During the search, the last valid route found is saved, so the router obtains the longest prefix match for the destination address.

Dynamic ARP

This part implements dynamic ARP, so the router does not depend only on a static ARP table. If the router does not find an ARP entry for the next hop, the packet is placed in a queue and an ARP Request is sent.
When an ARP Reply is received, the router stores the IP-to-MAC association in the ARP table and sends the packets that were waiting for that address.
In the `subiect3.png` screenshot, the ARP Request and ARP Reply packets appear before the ICMP packets. These packets are used to discover the required MAC addresses. After the addresses are obtained, the packet is forwarded correctly and the ping reply is received.

ICMP

This part implements ICMP functionality in the router. If a packet is sent directly to the router and it is an Echo Request, the router replies with an Echo Reply.
If there is no available route to the destination, or if the TTL expires, the router sends the corresponding ICMP error message.
In the `subiect4.png` screenshot, the command `ping -t 1` is used, meaning that the packet is sent with TTL 1. In Wireshark, the ICMP Echo Request can be seen, followed by the ICMP Time Exceeded message sent by the router. The same message also appears in the terminal, showing that the TTL expiration case is handled correctly.
